e edges and the edge  weights for the two and the fake hedges  they're exponential but with different  rates  now when there is an underlying tooth  planted but unknown then you have  some  natural statistical inference problems  that arise which is how well can you  recover the planet under matching  and  a natural solution is to consider okay  what is the  minimum  weight matching estimator and hatch how  does it perform statistically under this  planted model and this problem was  studied earlier by uh mohrami mohr and  sheep where they showed that  the shark statistical limit  happens uh at lambda equals four exactly  so that means when lamina is smaller  than four you have uh  um the minimum weight matching has to  suffer a constant fraction of  edge errors  and when lamb is larger than 4 the error  goes to 0.  so now with the  minimum weight matching cleanly analyzed  natural next step is to ask okay can we  do better statistically than the minimum  weight matching  and  the answer is actually you cannot do  better so the  minimum wage matching is the  statistically optimal and this is  answered in our paper this year  and we showed that lambda equals four is  indeed the sharp statistical threshold  for recovery  and um  in fact we are in our analysis we're no  longer restricted to considering only  exponential edge weights instead we can  allow the two edge weights and the fake  edge weights to follow arbitrary  distributions p and q  and  we not only consider  the complete graphs um but we actually  consider also  sparse graphs as well by assuming that  the edges on the fake edges they only  appear with probability d over n where d  can be as small as a constant which  corresponds to the sparse graphs and it  can be as large as all the way up to n  which corresponds to complete graphs  and we show that the statistical limit  for the recovery problem here depends  only on the average degree d  and the body chart coefficient  also known as the hellinger affinity  between p and q  and in the special case where the edge  weights are exponential and you have a  complete graph this statistical limit  collapses to lambda equals  which is attained by the minimum weight  matching  moreover we also show that near this  statistical image so when lambda is  equal to 4 minus some small epsilon  we're able to characterize the average  weight of the minimum  uh  sorry the average proportion of  mismatched edges  to be  scaling as e to the minus some small  constant  divided by square epsilon which shows  that the phase transition here is  infinitely smooth  now  so this result um  in a way it more or less gives a  complete answer to the sheriff's ratio  for the recovery problem under the  planted matching model  which makes me happy  however  uh more importantly i really think uh  what interests me most is uh the proof  strategies that are  um associated with this type of problems  and in fact we're analyzing or trying to  obtain with that obtain this result  first  um we tried some more conventional  methods including the second movement  method and also rate discharge arguments  and all these methods in the end they um  ended up failing  for us  and here is sort of a cartoon explaining  um the challenge in the analysis  basically to obtain a short statistical  image you are forced to look at the  posterior distribution  in particular you must look at  what is the um  posterior mass of  those matchings that are a fair distance  away from the shoes and because those  matchings um they potentially share a  large number of edges and the  interactions one stream ended up being  just overwhelming for us  and that means we had to develop some  new proof strategies in order to obtain  a  sharp analysis on the posterior  distribution  now um i'd like to take a step back from  here um  so um as a lot of you know there are  actually a lot of other planted problems  that are like this  um and the common theme of these  problems is really  there's  um so the shoe hidden signal  um does not it's not really represented  by some low-rank structure  and that means you cannot rely on some  more traditional methods like  spectral analysis or sdp relaxation and  this really brings in uh some new  challenges in both statistical analysis  and algorithmic design and really  i feel like that's a good thing because  it leaves a lot of room for researchers  to come up with our own  um new proof strategies  and uh another thing i like about this  type of problem is it's usually proved  by pictures combined with the  combinatorial arguments so overall it's  just a really fun playground to be in  finally i would like to just  make a short comment about the  computational gaps so for this  particular problem uh there is actually  no computational gap  because we know that the sharp threshold  is obtained by the minimum weight  matching or it can be  found via the hungarian algorithm in  polynomial time  however this is not true for  a lot of the other planet problems  listed and  for a lot of those problems there are  conjecture computational gaps  and it is still largely unknown how to  show those contracts your computational  gaps  and that's what i'm here to learn  thank you  i'm not sure i haven't looked at that  problem closely yeah but i i think some  of our  analysis techniques they may be applied  to the detection problem but at the  moment i can't tell you what's uh  sorry guys  unavoidably  um  you

